    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently looking at a collection of ceramic sherds from a Middle Bronze Age stratum. I'm having some trouble distinguishing between vessel types based solely on the firing techniques used. Could you explain the primary ways I can differentiate between oxidized and reduced firing in the clay fabric and the surface slip?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To distinguish between oxidized and reduced firing, look for dark, matte ash-like residue on the clay fabric in reduced flames, which creates a sooty, porous surface. In contrast, oxidized firing results in a brownish-yellow surface with distinct fire-scoring cracks that catch more light. Regarding slips, careful screening is essential because little burnt slip will often generate a light grey, charred dust during processing. Note that oxidized slips may sometimes appear darker or darker-yellow due to iron oxide effects during heating. These visual differences in texture and color should help your vessel identification.",
